Q: C Struct Passed By Value Memory Addresses in Called Function A: <p>I think you're trying to make things too complicated.  Take structures out of the picture for a moment. Suppose you have:</p>
<pre><code>int i = 1;
int j = 2;
printf(&quot;%p\n&quot;, (void *)&amp;i);
i = j;
printf(&quot;%p\n&quot;, (void *)&amp;i);
</code></pre>
<p>Do you expect two different addresses to be printed?  You shouldn't. Assignment changes the <em>value</em> of the object designated by the left-hand operand, not its address.</p>
<p>In fact, every object<sup>*</sup> has a characteristic address that remains the same throughout its lifetime.  That's part of the object's identity.  This is true regardless of the object's data type, so for structs just as much as for <code>int</code>s.  For unions and arrays, too, though arrays are a bit tricky for other reasons.</p>
<p>In comments, you wrote that you expected different because</p>
<blockquote>
<p>in my dealings with other languages that's the way it works.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>... but that expresses a misconception.  It does not work that way in any language you're likely to have come across, and in the languages you're thinking of, you probably can't even express the same thing.</p>
<p>C is a bit unusual among high(-ish)-level programming languages in that it gives you access both to individual objects and to their addresses.  Contrast with, for example,</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Java, which gives you only direct access to some data entities<sup>**</sup> (those having primitive types) and only indirect access to the rest (those having reference types).  A Java reference is analogous to a C pointer.  There is no way in Java to access entities of reference type directly, such as to assign one to another. One can touch only references to these.  There is therefore no good Java analog to the C code presented in the question.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Python, which doesn't give you direct access to <em>any</em> object.  You can interact only with references to objects, which again are analogous to C pointers.  There is therefore no good Python analog to the C code presented in the question.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Consider this C code:</p>
<pre><code>struct example {
    int i, j;
};

struct example ex1 = { 1, 2 };
struct example ex2;
struct example *p1 = &amp;ex1;
struct example *p2 = &amp;ex2;

printf(&quot;variable: %3s; address: %p\n&quot;, &quot;ex1&quot;, (void *) &amp;ex1);
printf(&quot;variable: %3s; address: %p\n&quot;, &quot;ex2&quot;, (void *) &amp;ex2);
printf(&quot;variable: %3s; address: %p\n&quot;, &quot;p1&quot;, (void *) &amp;p1);   // note: &amp;p1, not p1
printf(&quot;variable: %3s; address: %p\n&quot;, &quot;p2&quot;, (void *) &amp;p2);   // note: &amp;p2, not p2
printf(&quot;variable: %3s; value:   %p\n&quot;, &quot;p1&quot;, (void *) p1);    // note: p1, not &amp;p1
printf(&quot;variable: %3s; value:   %p\n&quot;, &quot;p2&quot;, (void *) p2);    // note: p2, not &amp;p2

ex2 = ex1;
printf(&quot;After ex2 = ex1:\n&quot;);
printf(&quot;variable: %3s; address: %p\n&quot;, &quot;ex2&quot;, (void *) &amp;ex2);

p2 = p1;
printf(&quot;After p2 = p1:\n&quot;);
printf(&quot;variable: %3s; address: %p\n&quot;, &quot;p2&quot;, (void *) &amp;p2);
printf(&quot;variable: %3s; value:   %p\n&quot;, &quot;p2&quot;, (void *) p2);
</code></pre>
<p>Output on my machine:</p>
<pre>variable: ex1; address: 0x7ffe40a3dd18
variable: ex2; address: 0x7ffe40a3dd10
variable:  p1; address: 0x7ffe40a3dd08
variable:  p2; address: 0x7ffe40a3dd00
variable:  p1; value:   0x7ffe40a3dd18
variable:  p2; value:   0x7ffe40a3dd10
After ex2 = ex1:
variable: ex2; address: 0x7ffe40a3dd10
After p2 = p1:
variable:  p2; address: 0x7ffe40a3dd00
variable:  p2; value:   0x7ffe40a3dd18
</pre>
<p>Observe that <code>ex1</code>, <code>ex2</code>, <code>p1</code> and <code>p2</code> all have distinct addresses, which are unchanged by assignment.  Observe also that the initial <em>value</em> of <code>p1</code> is the <em>address</em> of <code>ex1</code>, the initial value of <code>p2</code> is the address of <code>ex2</code>, and that after the assignment to <code>p2</code>, its new value is the same as the value of <code>p1</code> (the address of <code>ex1</code>).</p>
<p>In Java or Python, analogs of <code>p2 = p1</code> are the only option available for entities of compound types, but that's assignment of references, not assignment of the things to which the references refer.  What you are experimenting with in the code presented in the question corresponds to <code>ex2 = ex1</code>, which, again, has no close analogue in Java or Python.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>EDIT: I read somewhere that when doing assignments like think the compiler actually copies the fields, could it be that the fields get copied but the actual struct address does not?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think it's better to conceive of it in a uniform way: assignment (where allowed) writes new contents into the storage associated with the destination object.  That's the same for <code>int</code>s, <code>double</code>s, structures, and unions.  For structures, that storage comprises storage for all the structure members, so direct assignment to a structure writes into the storage for all the members.</p>
<p>In <code>ex1 = ex2</code>, the addresses of the strucures determine where the data are read from from and where they are written.  Of course the addresses themselves don't get copied.  They are not stored data (in objects <code>ex1</code> and <code>ex2</code>), they <em>identify</em> the stored data.</p>
<hr />
<p><sup>*</sup>Technically, every object whose address is ever computed or observed.</p>
<p><sup>**</sup>I avoid the term &quot;object&quot; here because Java terminology uses it differently than C terminology does.  In Java, &quot;object&quot; excludes primitives, but C does not have an analogous distinction between built-in data types and other data types.</p>

Q: Tab Focus Trapping with textArea causes trap to leave container A: <p>I went different paths, using events like <code>focus</code>, <code>focusout</code>, switching to the <code>tabindex</code> attribute etc. The only thing that worked was to clean up the HTML markup. I don't know if you have too many IDs (you have...) or if there was something else, but here you have a working example:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="false" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const container = document.forms.form01.specialinstructions;

const getFocusables = () =&gt; {
  return Array.from(container.elements)
    .sort((a, b) =&gt; (a.tabIndex ?? 9999) - (b.tabIndex ?? 9999));
};

container.addEventListener("keydown", (e) =&gt; {
  if (e.key !== "Tab") return;

  const focusables = getFocusables();
  if (focusables.length === 0) return;

  const first = focusables[0];
  const last = focusables[focusables.length - 1];

  // TAB (forward)
  if (!e.shiftKey &amp;&amp; document.activeElement === last) {
    first.focus();
    e.preventDefault();
  }
});</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>fieldset {
  border: none;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;form name="form01"&gt;
  &lt;fieldset name="specialinstructions"&gt;
    &lt;span&gt;Special Instructions&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;button tabindex="2"&gt;2&lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;button tabindex="3"&gt;3&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;textarea name="txt" maxlength="1020" rows="5" tabindex="1"&gt;&lt;/textarea&gt;
  &lt;/fieldset&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: PHP shorthand for isset &amp;&amp; truthy A: <p>I don't see any use for a shorter way for <code>$bar = isset($foo['bar']) &amp;&amp; $foo['bar'];</code> because this statement is hardly useful itself. What you probably meant is</p>
<pre><code>$bar = $foo['bar'] ?? null;
if (!$foo['bar']) {
    //do something terrible
}
</code></pre>
<p>or</p>
<pre><code>if (!isset($_POST['verylongname']) || !$_POST['verylongname']) {
    //do something terrible
}
$verylongname = $_POST['verylongname']);
</code></pre>
<p>The latter indeed could be shortened with empty(), but I wouldn't be a one-liner anyway.</p>
<p>The only one-liner I can think of is</p>
<pre><code>$verylongname = !empty($_POST['verylongname']) ? $_POST['verylongname'] : 'default value';
</code></pre>
<p>But still it duplicates the usage of verylongname which you probably want to avoid. In this case you can write such a helper yourself. For example</p>
<pre><code>function input_value($input, $value, $default = null) {
    return !empty($input[$value]) ? $input[$value] : $default;
}
</code></pre>
<p>And then have it your way</p>
<pre><code>$verylongname = input_value($_POST, 'verylongname');
</code></pre>
<p><strong>However,</strong> checking for just truthiness is considered a bad practice nowadays, for two reasons:</p>
<ul>
<li>just truthiness is it's too ambiguous. I can request your script with verylongname[]=foo, the check will be bypasses but you will get an error afterwards. Or it can be a legitimate 0 value but it will be rejected, etc. etc.</li>
<li>there are other validations that you'd likely want to add, the max length for example</li>
</ul>
<p>Therefore it's better to create (or adopt a ready made) validation library that does a more helpful validation than just checking for existence and truthiness.</p>

Q: Fixing Connection timed out in PHP way (on shared server) A: <p>You can do something like below.</p>
<pre><code>    class DatabaseConnection {
    private $conn = null;
    private $maxRetries = 3;
    private $retryDelay = 1; // seconds
    private $config;

    public function __construct($config) {
        $this-&gt;config = $config;
    }

    public function getConnection() {
        if ($this-&gt;conn === null) {
            $this-&gt;connect();
        }
        return $this-&gt;conn;
    }

    private function connect() {
        $this-&gt;conn = new mysqli(
            $this-&gt;config['host'],
            $this-&gt;config['username'],
            $this-&gt;config['password'],
            $this-&gt;config['database']
        );

        if ($this-&gt;conn-&gt;connect_error) {
            throw new Exception(&quot;Connection failed: &quot; . $this-&gt;conn-&gt;connect_error);
        }

        // Set session wait_timeout
        $this-&gt;conn-&gt;query(&quot;SET SESSION wait_timeout=28800&quot;); // 8 hours

        // Set session interactive_timeout
        $this-&gt;conn-&gt;query(&quot;SET SESSION interactive_timeout=28800&quot;);
    }

    public function query($sql, $params = []) {
        $attempts = 0;

        while ($attempts &lt; $this-&gt;maxRetries) {
            try {
                if (!$this-&gt;ping()) {
                    $this-&gt;reconnect();
                }

                if ($params) {
                    return $this-&gt;preparedQuery($sql, $params);
                } else {
                    $result = $this-&gt;conn-&gt;query($sql);
                    if ($result === false) {
                        throw new Exception($this-&gt;conn-&gt;error);
                    }
                    return $result;
                }

            } catch (Exception $e) {
                $attempts++;

                // Check if it's a connection error
                if ($this-&gt;isConnectionError($e-&gt;getMessage())) {
                    if ($attempts &lt; $this-&gt;maxRetries) {
                        $this-&gt;reconnect();
                        sleep($this-&gt;retryDelay);
                        continue;
                    }
                }

                throw $e;
            }
        }
    }

    private function preparedQuery($sql, $params) {
        $stmt = $this-&gt;conn-&gt;prepare($sql);
        if ($stmt === false) {
            throw new Exception($this-&gt;conn-&gt;error);
        }

        // Bind parameters
        $types = '';
        foreach ($params as $param) {
            if (is_int($param)) $types .= 'i';
            elseif (is_float($param)) $types .= 'd';
            else $types .= 's';
        }

        $stmt-&gt;bind_param($types, ...$params);

        if (!$stmt-&gt;execute()) {
            throw new Exception($stmt-&gt;error);
        }

        $result = $stmt-&gt;get_result();
        $stmt-&gt;close();

        return $result;
    }

    private function ping() {
        try {
            return $this-&gt;conn &amp;&amp; $this-&gt;conn-&gt;ping();
        } catch (Exception $e) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    private function reconnect() {
        $this-&gt;close();
        $this-&gt;connect();
    }

    private function close() {
        if ($this-&gt;conn) {
            $this-&gt;conn-&gt;close();
            $this-&gt;conn = null;
        }
    }

    private function isConnectionError($error) {
        $connectionErrors = [
            'server has gone away',
            'Connection timed out',
            'Lost connection',
            'is dead or not enabled',
            'Error while sending',
            'decryption failed or bad record mac',
            'SSL connection has been closed unexpectedly'
        ];

        foreach ($connectionErrors as $errorString) {
            if (stripos($error, $errorString) !== false) {
                return true;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }

    public function __destruct() {
        $this-&gt;close();
    }
}

// For example
try {
    $config = [
        'host' =&gt; 'your_host',
        'username' =&gt; 'your_username',
        'password' =&gt; 'your_password',
        'database' =&gt; 'your_database'
    ];

    $db = new DatabaseConnection($config);

    // Example of a simple query
    $result = $db-&gt;query(&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE active = ?&quot;, [1]);

    // Process results
    while ($row = $result-&gt;fetch_assoc()) {
        // Process each row
    }

} catch (Exception $e) {
    // Log the error
    error_log(&quot;Database error: &quot; . $e-&gt;getMessage());

    // Handle the error appropriately
    http_response_code(500);
    echo &quot;An error occurred. Please try again later.&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can also try connection pooling and query timeout handling</p>
